description:
I am using `rocker/verse` in my docker file and when I try to load pins, it gives me the same error from https://github.com/rstudio/pins-r/issues/705 

```
Error: package or namespace load failed for ‘pins’:
 .onLoad failed in loadNamespace() for 'pins', details:
  call: NULL
  error: [ENOENT] Failed to make directory '//.cache/pins': no such file or directory
Execution halted
```

I looked further and believe the error is from this function:

```
board_cache_path <- function(name) {
  # R_CONFIG_ACTIVE suggests we're in a production environment
  if (has_envvars("R_CONFIG_ACTIVE") || has_envvars("PINS_USE_CACHE")) {
    path <- fs::dir_create(tempdir(), "pins")
  } else if (has_envvars("PINS_CACHE_DIR") ) {
    path <- Sys.getenv("PINS_CACHE_DIR")
  } else {
    path <- cache_dir()
  }
  fs::path(path, name)
}
```

I guess rocker doesnt set `R_CONFIG_ACTIVE` so pins doesnt know it's in a production environment? In any case, setting `R_CONFIG_ACTIVE` as an environment variable in my heroku app makes the error go away. 

Hard to make a reprex but basically the same reprex suggested in https://github.com/rstudio/pins-r/issues/705  gives the error when deployed .

When uploading ImageNet partitioned to Google Cloud, the `data.txt` index does not properly update due to `Cache-Control` defaulting to one hour for thee `data.txt` file. 

A non-minimal reeprex:

```
for (path in dir("/localssd/imagenet/ILSVRC/Data/CLS-LOC/train/", full.names = TRUE)) {
  if (runif(1) > 0.9) pins::pin(mtcars, board = "gcloud")
  dir(path, full.names = TRUE) %>% pin(name = basename(path), board = "gcloud", zip = TRUE)
}
```

See [https://github.com/mlverse/imagenet](https://github.com/mlverse/imagenet/blob/master/README.md)

I've written what is essentially a small wrapper for `pin_find %>% nrow` but found it useful for if - else statements (e.g. when adding rows to existing pin via `pin_get %>% bind_rows %>% pin` versus new `pin`). I'd find it useful if something like this was available as an exported function from `pins`.

```
pin_exists <- function(name, board = "local") {
  output <- ifelse(nrow(pin_find(name = name, board = board))==0, FALSE, TRUE)
}
```

@javierluraschi pointed out to me in #170 that if you pass a vector of files to `pin()` and have set the threshold size `options(pins.github.release = 0)` to force all uploads to release assets, then they all go on the same release.  

I was thus surprised to see that if I pass a vector of objects to `pin` where I'm using the default size threshhold (and all objects are nice and small) when using a github_board, pin creates a new commit for each object.  It would be nice if possible to have all these files committed in a single commit.  This is probably relevant for versioning (#178) and more immediately, the current setup makes it very very slow for a large vector of small files.  (Though on the flip side, `pin()` with threshold set to 0 just fails when I try to send a lot of objects at once).  

(in other words: whoopsies, did I just make 2913 commits? 🙋‍♂ 😊 )

I'm not sure whether this is something that would be possible to include in the package. It's not clear to me how `cache = TRUE` works, but it would be nice to have some control over that. I.e. maybe take a `memoise::cache_filesystem()` argument or something (Related to #134 and #95).

Adding a dependency (or at least a `Suggests` dependency w/ some checks) on `memoise` in this naive implementation:

```
# implemented by #134 ?
pin_last_updated <- function(name, board) {
  as.POSIXlt(pin_info(name = name, board = board)$last_deployed_time, format = "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
}

# downside... this only memoises a particular pin_get
memoise_pin_get <- function(name, board, cache = memoise::cache_memory()) {
  memoise::memoise(
    function() pin_get(name = name, board = board, cache = FALSE),
    ~ pin_last_updated(name = name, board = board),
    cache = cache
  )
}

mypin <- memoise_pin_get("iris", board = "rsconnect", cache = memoise::cache_filesystem(".pin_cache"))

# use
mypin()
```

This helps with persistence across R sessions, performance / redundancy if a pin does not update often, could help with the offline context in #95 , etc.
